  "body": "In this flat the three roommates repeatedly clash over chores because agreements are made verbally, schedules shift, and deeper feelings of resentment and power imbalance surface. One roommate is now avoiding conversations, which may signal burnout or simply an unwillingness to confront the tension. The earlier contributions highlighted two main strands: (1) the need for clear, documented coordination (claims r1‑r3) and (2) the importance of addressing relational dynamics (objections r8‑r11). The proposed tools—a shared digital chore board, a brief weekly check‑in, and a set of non‑verbal cues—offer procedural clarity while providing low‑pressure ways to signal workload and emotional state. However, objections remind us that a board can become a policing instrument (objection r9) and that any system must be built on a culture of non‑blame (objection r14). The open problem remains how to embed emotional safety into the coordination tool so that clarity does not create new conflict. The commitments below aim to integrate these insights: a board with explicit ground‑rules, a rotating weekly check‑in, visual cues for “busy” or “need help,” and an escalation step if relational tension persists after a trial period.",
  "commitments": "{"language":"FCL-1","records":[
    {"id":"r21","type":"claim","text":"Both vague, undocumented chore agreements and underlying relational dynamics (power imbalances, accumulated resentment, differing conflict styles) drive the household disputes.","depends":["p.carry.1#r1","p.carry.2#r8"]},
    {"id":"r22","type":"objection","text":"A shared digital chore board can turn into a policing device unless clear, non‑blame ground‑rules and a supportive culture are established.","depends":["p.carry.2#r9"]},
    {"id":"r23","type":"problem","text":"How can the household embed relational safety and emotional openness into a chore‑coordination tool so that procedural clarity does not generate new conflict?"},
    {"id":"r24","type":"commitment","text":"Set up a shared digital chore board (e.g., Trello or Google Sheet) that lists all recurring tasks, allows anyone to claim, swap, or edit slots, and includes a visible log of changes. Attach explicit ground‑rules: no blame language, updates are informational only, and any concern immediately triggers a brief feelings check‑in.","scope":"household","action":"install board and publish rules","depends":["r21","r22"]},
    {"id":"r25","type":"commitment","text":"Hold a 10‑minute weekly chore check‑in after dinner with a rotating facilitator. Agenda: task status, overload alerts, and a 2‑minute space for each person to share how they feel about the workload.","scope":"household","action":"schedule weekly check‑in","depends":["p.carry.0#r17","p.carry.2#r11"]},
    {"id":"r26","type":"commitment","text":"Introduce a non‑verbal cue system on the board (colored stickers or emoji tags) that lets any roommate indicate \"busy\" or \"need help\" without direct conversation.","scope":"household","action":"add cue icons to board","depends":["p.carry.0#r18"]},
    {"id":"r27","type":"use","text":"Use the digital board together with the cue system to track chore completion and signal real‑time assistance needs.","scope":"ongoing","action":"track chores and cues","depends":["r24","r26"]},
    {"id":"r28","type":"commitment","text":"If after two weeks relational tension remains high, convene a mediated discussion with a neutral third party or reconsider the living arrangement.","scope":"household","action":"plan escalation step","depends":["p.carry.0#r20","p.carry.2#r12"]}
